Scenario A — Curriculum Server authentication

A platform administrator requests an OTP, receives it through the configured channel, verifies it, and completes the authentication workflow.

Scenario B — School administrator authentication

A school administrator requests an OTP. The system resolves the correct school tenant and delivers the OTP through the school's configured authentication policy.

Scenario C — Curriculum Flow authentication

A user authenticates through a provisioned Curriculum Flow instance. The correct tenant and application context are enforced, and the OTP is delivered through the central gateway.

Scenario D — Parent attendance notification

A real attendance event triggers a notification to the verified and authorized parent/guardian through the configured SMS or WhatsApp channel.

Scenario E — Fee notification

A real fee invoice or payment event triggers the correct school notification without exposing another student's or tenant's information.

Scenario F — Emergency broadcast

An authorized school administrator sends an emergency broadcast to the permitted recipient group, with delivery tracking and a complete audit trail.

Scenario G — Provider failure

The primary provider becomes unavailable. The system records the failure, applies the configured fallback policy, and reports the final result accurately.

Scenario H — Tenant isolation

School A attempts to access School B's configuration, messages, delivery history, and usage data. All unauthorized access is denied.

Scenario I — Provisioning

A new school is provisioned with SMS and WhatsApp enabled. The service registers the required entitlements, configuration, templates, policies, and application connections.

Scenario J — Curriculum Flow provisioning

A new Curriculum Flow instance is provisioned and automatically receives its authorized connection to the central messaging service.

